<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Schwarzenberg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Schwarzenberg family</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Schwarzenberg is a Franconian noble family that rose in the service of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and became princes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in 1670. It acquired large estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesky-krumlov">Český Krumlov</a> and Hluboká, and gained the Klettgau by marriage in 1687. Members included Field Marshal Karl Philipp, allied commander at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in 1813, and Felix, Austrian prime minister from 1848 to 1852.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-schwarzenberg.jpg" alt="House of Schwarzenberg" width="320" height="357" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SVG original: MostEpic Edited by: Janschuster · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_the_House_of_Schwarzenberg_(Primary).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The family descends from the Lords of Seinsheim, established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Early in the 15th century Erkinger of Seinsheim acquired the territory and castle of Schwarzenberg at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheinfeld">Scheinfeld</a>, and in 1429 he was made Freiherr, or Baron, of Schwarzenberg; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> tower on a black hill was then added to the family arms. The house was raised to Imperial Counts in 1599, when Adolf von Schwarzenberg received a quarter showing a raven pecking at a Turk's head, for his capture of the Turkish-held fortress of Győr, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raba">Raab</a> in German, on 19 March 1598.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Marriages brought the house its lands. Count Georg Ludwig gained the Styrian lordship of Murau in 1623, and an alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-eggenberg">House of Eggenberg</a> gave it large holdings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> in 1661; in the 1670s the family made Bohemia its main seat, and until 1918 it lived chiefly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesky-krumlov">Český Krumlov</a>. Johann Ludwig II, Count of Sulz, having no male heir, arranged for his rights and property to pass to Prince Ferdinand of Schwarzenberg, his daughter's husband, on his death in 1687. The family later became Dukes of Krumlov, in 1719, and in Bohemia also acquired the property of the House of Rosenberg.</p><p>Early in the 19th century the house divided into two princely lines, as Prince Ferdinand's will had foreseen: a senior line holding the Palais Schwarzenberg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, Krumlov and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hluboka-nad-vltavou">Frauenberg</a>, and a cadet line founded by Field Marshal Karl Philipp at Orlík Castle. For his victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leipzig">Battle of the Nations</a>, where he commanded the Sixth Coalition, the Austrian emperor allowed him to add the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> arms with an upright sword to his own.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> was set up in 1939, the Nazi authorities seized the possessions of Prince Adolph of Schwarzenberg, and his cousin Heinrich, Duke of Krumlov, was arrested and deported. Czechoslovak law No. 143/1947 of 13 August 1947, the Lex Schwarzenberg, gave the assets of the senior line to the Land of Bohemia. That line died out in the male line in 1979, and the two branches were reunited under Karel Schwarzenberg (1937–2023), who served as Czech Minister of Foreign Affairs. Eighteen members of the family were knights of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-the-golden-fleece">Order of the Golden Fleece</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Schwarzenberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1806 Klettgau was mediatised and placed under the Grand Duchy of Baden, which later bought out the <b>Schwarzenbergs</b>' remaining rights.</blockquote><a href="/state/landgraviate-of-klettgau">Landgraviate of Klettgau</a></li></ul></section>
